use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Dragging import Dragging

export ui-component Splitter:
    description:
        A splitter divides an area into two or more panels, side by side or
        stacked, with a handle between each pair. Dragging a handle makes the
        panel on one side bigger and the one on the other side smaller, like
        the divider between a file list and a preview.

    behaviors:
        - @{Dragging}

    sizes:
        Each panel's size is a share of the whole area, and the shares always
        add up to all of it. The component sets the starting sizes, and can set
        sizes itself later. Whether sizes can also be given in fixed units is
        up to the chosen library.

    limits:
        The component can give each panel a smallest and largest size, and a
        handle stops when either panel next to it reaches one. Whether panels
        further along then give way is up to the chosen library.

    keyboard:
        Each handle is its own stop in the Tab order. The arrow keys in the
        splitter's direction move the handle by a small step, and Home and End
        move it as far as it will go each way. A bigger step with a modifier
        key, and a key to jump between panels, are up to the chosen library.

    collapsing:
        The component can let a panel collapse. Dragging its handle past the
        panel's smallest size snaps it shut, to nothing or to a thin strip, and
        dragging back reopens it. Pressing Enter on the handle collapses the
        panel or restores it to the size it had before.

    names:
        Each handle has a name saying which panel it resizes, such as "Resize
        sidebar". Assistive tools read out its position as a value between its
        limits.

    direction:
        Panels can sit side by side or be stacked. Side by side, they are laid
        out and moved right to left for right-to-left languages.

    reporting:
        The component is told the sizes continuously while a handle moves, and
        again once when the user lets go, so it can remember them for next
        time. It is also told when a panel collapses or reopens.

    nesting:
        A panel can hold another splitter running the other way. Dragging
        where two handles cross to resize both at once is up to the chosen
        library.

    nonGoals:
        - No dragging panels to rearrange or dock them somewhere else
        - Doesn't remember sizes between visits. The component can store what
            it is told
        - Not a Slider, even though a handle has a value
